                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgDslAhzkZu_Oq6rigZLlCaVn-tfCOa1vu3ybROAgHuWdrNTjeWjdAqX6_6ZHMIDNlYg6MmxdA-NciCoeOAwGWdq2GoeHsO1qvCXbrDMAMDkl9uJ5b2cR0cMQAqoH-hEAjvSIA1y6Z3kqp31L-8KS0qvM7hdLfxbLCtu17Ke766hvMmqsGR26fCLqJtnaA/s1718/USTreasuryRates2026.jpg",
                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran revisits the long-running debate over whether rising interest rates necessarily drag down stock prices, using 2026 as a live case study. U.S. ten-year Treasury yields climbed to 4.75% by August 2026, yet the S\u0026P 500 held firm thanks to strong earnings growth among large-cap companies. He argues that the Fed does not set the trajectory of rates ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â fundamentals do ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and that rate impacts are highly asymmetric across sectors. Energy stocks benefited while consumer-facing businesses with thin margins and heavy debt faced the most pressure.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s central point ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â that rates are a symptom of growth conditions, not a blunt hammer on equities ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â is a useful corrective to reflexive \u0027rates up, stocks down\u0027 thinking. For investors, the practical implication is to stop managing against the Fed and start managing against balance-sheet quality: high-debt, low-margin businesses remain structurally vulnerable regardless of whether the next Fed move is a cut or a hold. Rotate toward free-cash-flow compounders in energy and large-cap tech, and treat consumer cyclicals with caution until rate relief actually materializes in household spending data.",
