                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran argues that venture capital\u0027s structural incentives create a systemic bias toward revenue scaling over sustainable profitability, which he labels the industry\u0027s \u0027weakest link.\u0027 He shows that aggressive expansion only succeeds under a narrow set of conditions ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â large addressable markets, low capital intensity, and strong unit economics ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and that many VC-backed companies lack all three. The result is a class of \u0027Field of Nightmares\u0027 businesses that consume capital indefinitely without converging on profit. The piece traces how founder incentives, LP pressure for large exits, and availability of cheap capital have collectively hardwired this dysfunction.",
                                                                                "take":  "This piece is a timely warning for anyone evaluating late-stage private companies or recent IPOs still trading on growth multiples. The AI infrastructure wave is producing a new cohort of scale-first, profit-optional businesses that closely fit Damodaran\u0027s \u0027Field of Nightmares\u0027 template. Enterprise buyers and investors alike should demand unit-economics transparency ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â customer acquisition cost, payback period, gross margin at scale ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â before accepting a revenue-growth story. Companies that cannot articulate a credible path to positive free cash flow within a defined time horizon should be treated as speculative positions, sized accordingly.",
