                                                                                "date":  "2026-08-02",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/edc330cd-79d0-4826-a3a5-bf12f41ad6a3/alex_kantrowitz.png?t=1785677394",
                                                                                "summary":  "Josh Brown interviews Big Technology reporter Alex Kantrowitz, who argues that fears of AI data center overbuilding are misplaced ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the real concern among tech leaders is that there is not nearly enough compute to support the coming era of billions of autonomous AI agents. The piece contends that demand from agentic AI, autonomous vehicles, and robotics will dwarf current infrastructure capacity by orders of magnitude. Brown frames this as an argument for continued and accelerating capital investment in AI infrastructure despite near-term market skepticism about hyperscaler capex.",
                                                                                "take":  "The bear case on AI infrastructure spending assumes today\u0027s use cases represent the ceiling. Brown and Kantrowitz are arguing the opposite: today\u0027s GPU clusters are the floor. For investors, this strengthens the long-term case for companies across the AI compute stack ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â power, cooling, networking, semiconductor equipment ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â even as near-term earnings multiples look stretched. Enterprise leaders should read this as a signal to accelerate their own AI infrastructure roadmaps rather than waiting for costs to fall ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the companies that secure capacity and talent now will have a durable structural advantage over late movers.",
